    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22144": {
      "title": "Dalhousie University",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Limited Term Research Assistance &amp; Subject Liaison Librarian Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Limited Term Research Assistance & Subject Liaison Librarian Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Limited Term Research Assistance & Subject Liaison Librarian Posting Number F813P Type of position Limited Term Department/Unit Killam Library Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada About the opportunity About the opportunity The Dalhousie Libraries seek a collaborative, flexible, innovative and service-oriented professional librarian for a full-time, 12-month term position with an anticipated start date of November 1st, 2026. Appointed to the Killam Memorial Library and reporting to the Dean of Libraries through the Head, Library Academic and Research Services, the incumbent will assist in the provision of a range of liaison librarian services to support teaching, learning, research and outreach to support the Faculty of Arts & Social Sciences. About Dalhousie University Libraries Located on Canada’s East Coast, with campuses in the vibrant oceanside city of Halifax and an Agricultural Campus in the town of Truro, Dalhousie welcomes 21,000 students from across the country and more than 115 countries around the world. Dalhousie is Nova Scotia’s leading research-intensive university, with 13 faculties that expand understanding through teaching excellence and a drive for discovery. Dalhousie Libraries enhance learning and discovery for our students and community through our extensive collections, innovative services, and vibrant learning environments. Dalhousie Libraries’ system comprises five library locations and two learning commons across the university’s four campuses, and includes archival collections, a GIS (Geographic Information Systems) centre, and a copyright office. Visit Dalhousie Libraries ’ website for more information. The Dalhousie Libraries is a member of Novanet , a consortium of Nova Scotia academic libraries sharing a common library management system (Alma), the Council of Atlantic Academic Libraries ( CAAL - CBPA ), and the Canadian Association of Research Libraries ( CARL ), among other collaborative initiatives. The Role: As a subject liaison librarian for Political Science, International Development Studies, Black and African Diaspora Studies, College of Sustainability, Classics and Arabic Studies, the incumbent provides a range of liaison services including collections development, library instruction in a variety of formats and research assistance, working closely with colleagues from all libraries in the Dalhousie Libraries system. Instructional and research assistance sessions may be in-person, hybrid or online. Main Areas of Responsibility: Serve as liaison librarian for the following departments and programs in the Faculty of Arts & Social Sciences: Political Science, International Development Studies, Black and African Diaspora Studies, College of Sustainability, Classics and Arabic Studies. Provide reference and information services including reference & research consultations to students, faculty and researchers. Develop and deliver information research instruction in in-person, hybrid and online settings, applying active learning methods as well as innovative educational technologies. Collaborate with colleagues to ensure collection, research needs and learning outcomes are met. Complete course and program collection assessments as needed. Plan and create digital learning objects including tutorials, learning modules and instructional materials, for the use of students and faculty. Seek opportunities to engage faculty to increase their awareness of information literacy opportunities and benefits. Instruct and assist clients on site and remotely to effectively discover, access and use the full range of library and information resources available to meet teaching, learning and research needs. Contribute to the Dal Libraries’ strategic initiatives through participation in various projects and committees, such as teaching and learning, scholarly communication and research data management. Actively engage in continuing education/professional development. Required Qualifications: MI/ MLIS / MLS degree from an ALA accredited program or equivalent. A strong commitment to public service and excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Knowledge of relevant information resources in the liaison areas and how to deploy them in support of research and teaching. Ability to work successfully with a wide variety of people and to promote respect and inclusion. Experience presenting online or in-person library instructional material in a clear and well-designed manner. Experience providing research support or reference in an academic or special library. Preferred Qualifications: Undergraduate education in the social sciences or equivalent experience providing liaison services in the social sciences in an academic or special library. Basic understanding of how to approach/navigate research questions in the sciences. Knowledge of or experience with library acquisitions portals (e.g. GOBI , Rialto), as well as online services platforms (e.g. LibGuides) Awareness of professional issues and current trends in research and instruction. Experience managing multiple projects concurrently, setting and meeting deadlines and adapting to new and changing situations, priorities, and technologies. Experience supporting evidence synthesis projects. Salary and Benefits: Salary is dependent upon qualifications and experience and is subject to the terms of the Dalhousie Faculty Association Collective Agreement . The position will not be filled at a rank higher than Librarian 1. Salary scales for Professional Librarian members for 2026-2027 are set out in Schedule G of the agreement. The salary range for Librarian 1 is $74,171 – $81,854. Availability of the Position: The position has a proposed starting date of 1 November 2026. Deadline for Applications: September 27, 2026 Application Instructions: Applications will be submitted through the PeopleAdmin portal: https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22051 . A complete application will include: a cover letter outlining qualifications a curriculum vitae the contact information for at least three referees with knowledge of your work (i.e. immediate supervisors) Assessment of applications will begin after the closing date and continue until a suitable candidate is found. The appointment is expected to commence November 1, 2026 or as negotiated. This position is a part of the Dalhousie Faculty Association ( DFA ) Bargaining Unit. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Open Date 09/04/2026 Close Date 09/27/2026 Open Until Filled No Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22051 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover Letter List of referees Optional Documents Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22147": {
      "title": "Dalhousie University",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Learning Platforms Analyst Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Learning Platforms Analyst Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Learning Platforms Analyst Research Project Department/Unit OLCD Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Posting Number S242-26 Employee Group NSGEU Local 77 Position Type Term Duration of Contract (if applicable) 1 year Employment Type Full Time Full-time Equivalency (FTE) 1.0 Salary $34.37-$43.52 per hour ($58,087 - $73,556 per annum, 32.5 hours per week) Classification SIT1 Provisional Statement This is a provisional classification. Formal regular review classification procedures may be initiated at any time by the incumbent or supervisor after the incumbent has worked a minimum of six months in the role. About Dalhousie University Dalhousie University is Atlantic Canada’s leading research-intensive university and a driver of the region’s intellectual, social and economic development. Located in the heart of Halifax, Nova Scotia, with an Agricultural Campus in Truro/Bible Hill, Dalhousie is a truly national and international university, with more than half of our almost 21,000 students coming from outside of the province. Our 6,000 faculty and staff foster a vibrant, purpose-driven community, that celebrated 200 years of academic excellence in 2018. Job Summary The Faculty of Open Learning and Career Development ( OLCD ) develops and delivers innovative and diverse lifelong learning experiences for more than 5000 students annually to help them navigate the changing world of learning and work. Reporting to the Manager, Faculty Technology, the Learning Platforms Analyst supports and enhances the Faculty’s learning and credentialing platforms, including Modern Campus Lifelong Learning Extended Education ( LLL EE), MyCreds, and D2L Brightspace. The role provides operational and user support, monitors, integrations, coordinates onboarding for participating units, and supports credentialing initiatives across certificates, transcripts and microcredentials . This role requires someone who can support users and platforms day-to-day while also analyzing workflows, monitoring integrations, troubleshooting data issues, coordinating testing, supporting credentialing expansion, and onboarding new units into a standardized learning and credentialing ecosystem. Key Responsibilities Provide operational support for learning platforms, including user account management, access provisioning, application configuration, and Learning Management System ( LMS ) administration. Monitor and maintain system integrations between Modern Campus LLL EE, Banner, MyCreds, and related systems, resolving data quality, identity, and provisioning issues. Serve as the primary operational and functional resource for expansion of the LLL EE/MyCreds integration to encompass transcripts and certificates, supporting credential configuration, data mapping, testing, exception handling, go-live readiness, and operational handover. Manage onboarding and implementation projects for new continuing education units, including requirements gathering, workflow design, testing, training, and post-launch support. Coordinate stakeholders and vendor relationships, serving as the primary liaison among Faculty of Open Learning and Career Development at Dal ( FOLCD ), the Registrar’s Office, ITS , Modern Campus, MyCreds, and participating academic units to coordinate requirements, issue resolution, vendor escalation, and implementation activities. Drive continuous improvement and user enablement by developing procedures, documentation, training materials, operational standards, and recommendations for process platform, credentialing, automation, and integration improvements. Note The successful applicant will be eligible for hybrid work (combination of in-person work on campus and remote work from within Nova Scotia) as agreed by all parties based on operational requirements and university guidelines. Required Qualifications Undergraduate degree in a relevant field such as information management, business administration, computer science, education/learning technology, information systems, or a related discipline, plus approximately one to five years’ experience supporting enterprise platforms, users, integrations, configuration, testing, documentation, or implementation activities (or an equivalent combination of education, training, and experience). Demonstrated experience in systems analysis, troubleshooting, platform configuration, documentation, and user training/support. Experience supporting enterprise applications, including system integrations, data management processes, identity resolution, access provisioning, or related operational workflows. Experience planning and executing testing activities, including system, integration, and user acceptance testing. Ability to deliver user training and support platform adoption across diverse stakeholder groups. Experience coordinating projects, implementation plans, timelines, and stakeholder communications Assets Experience with testing, validation, change management, release cycles, and vendor support. Experience with platforms such as Modern Campus LLL EE (Destiny One), D2L Brightspace, Ellucian Banner, and/or ARUCC MyCreds. Experience with credential issuance, digital credentials, microcredentials, certificates, transcripts, or learner records. Familiarity with privacy, accessibility, records management, or data governance considerations in a post-secondary environment. Job Competencies The successful candidate is expected to demonstrate proficiency in Dalhousie’s core competencies, in particular: - Self-Awareness & Professionalism - Integrity - Respect & Inclusion - Adaptability - Knowledge & Thinking Skills Additional Information Dalhousie University supports a healthy and balanced lifestyle. Our total compensation package includes a defined benefit pension plan, health and dental plans, a health spending account, an employee and family assistance program and a tuition assistance program. Application Consideration Non-probationary bargaining unit employees ( NSGEU local 77) are given priority consideration. All other applications will be considered when there is no qualified candidate from the bargaining unit. We sincerely appreciate all applications and note that only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted directly by the hiring department or People and Culture. Diversity Statement Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Open Date 09/08/2026 Close Date 09/15/2026 Open Until Filled No Special Instructions to Applicant Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/21886 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Optional Documents Cover Letter Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
