    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22154": {
      "title": "DFA Instructor, 1.0 FTE Limited Term Contract (1 Year)",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | DFA Instructor, 1.0 FTE Limited Term Contract (1 Year) Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants DFA Instructor, 1.0 FTE Limited Term Contract (1 Year) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title DFA Instructor, 1.0 FTE Limited Term Contract (1 Year) Posting Number F794P Type of position Limited Term Department/Unit Occupational Therapy Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada About the opportunity The School of Occupational Therapy in Dalhousie’s Faculty of Health invites applications from qualified candidates for a 12-month full-time limited-term faculty position at the Instructor rank (subject to final budgetary approval). This academic position encompasses teaching, teaching support, lab coordination, fieldwork teaching, and fieldwork support in the MSc(OT) entry-to-practice program. This position reports to the Dean of the Faculty of Health through the Director of the School of Occupational Therapy. There is no expectation of a research workload associated with this position. The position is available to begin immediately following the completion of the search process, or at a mutually agreed-upon date. Dalhousie University is comprised of 6,000 faculty and staff among its 13 faculties. Current enrollment is 21,000+ graduate and undergraduate students across four campuses. As part of Canada’s U15 research-intensive group, Dalhousie University is an influential driver of the region’s intellectual, social, and economic development. It is the largest university in Atlantic Canada and is located on the ancestral and unceded territory of the Mi’kmaq in the heart of Halifax (Kjipuktuk). This scenic coastal city is the capital of Nova Scotia. Nova Scotia is home to 13 Mi’kmaq First Nations, a deeply rooted African Nova Scotia community, and an increasingly diverse population which also includes a vibrant workforce of people living with (dis)Abilities. The Faculty of Health’s faculty, staff, and students are committed to improving the well-being of individuals, families, communities, and populations through diverse health programming, collaborative research, and strong community partnerships. The Faculty of Health is one of the largest faculties at Dalhousie, comprised of eight schools, one college, and one program, over 1,000 employees, and more than 3,000 students. With the most programmatically diverse health faculty in Canada, offering 45 academic programs, diplomas, and certificates at the entry, graduate, and advanced professional levels, our students are exposed to a broad range of health topics and learning experiences. Known for its theory-driven approach, commitment to social justice, and diverse educational approaches, the School of Occupational Therapy’s vision is advancing well-being in everyday life through occupation, leadership, community, and challenging the status quo. Key responsibilities of the position include developing and delivering course content, instructing and evaluating students across the Master of Science in Occupational Therapy program. The role also supports the Fieldwork Program and related initiatives, contributes to interprofessional health education, and assists with lab coordination, tutor training, and teaching support activities such as student evaluations and simulated client exams. Additional duties may include contributing to administrative functions within the School of Occupational Therapy and the Faculty of Health. Candidates must hold an entry-level degree in Occupational Therapy (a Master’s degree is preferred) and be licensed with the Nova Scotia Occupational Therapy Regulator ( NSOTR ), with at least three years of clinical experience, one year of teaching-related experience, and experience conducting and supporting research projects. They should demonstrate strong knowledge of current competencies, equity-focused and anti-oppressive teaching practices, the ability to support fieldwork education and preceptor development, and the ability to teach research courses and supervise MScOT student research projects. Excellent communication, organization, leadership, and time management skills are essential, along with a commitment to professional development. Preferred qualifications include proficiency with online platforms such as Brightspace, demonstrated ability in course development, lab coordination, simulation, and student evaluation methods such as OSCEs. Applications must include a cover letter stating qualifications and experience; a Curriculum Vitae; a statement of teaching experience and interests; copies of teaching evaluations (if available); and a copy of license with the NSOTR (upload as “Portfolio”). For further information regarding this position, contact: Dr. Paula Rushton, Director School of Occupational Therapy Faculty of Health prushton@dal.ca This position is a part of the Dalhousie Faculty Association ( DFA ) Bargaining Unit. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Open Date 08/11/2026 Close Date 10/04/2026 Open Until Filled No Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/21491 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover Letter Teaching Statement Portfolio List of referees Optional Documents Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22167": {
      "title": "Associate University Veterinarian",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Associate University Veterinarian Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Associate University Veterinarian Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Associate University Veterinarian Research Project Department/Unit University Veterinarian Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Posting Number S293-26 Employee Group DPMG Position Type Term Duration of Contract (if applicable) 1 year Employment Type Full Time Full-time Equivalency (FTE) 1.0 Salary $94,506 - $127,448 per annum Classification ADM-08 Provisional Statement This is a provisional classification. Formal regular review classification procedures may be initiated at any time by the incumbent or supervisor after the incumbent has worked a minimum of six months in the role. About Dalhousie University Dalhousie University is Atlantic Canada’s leading research-intensive university and a driver of the region’s intellectual, social and economic development. Located in the heart of Halifax, Nova Scotia, with an Agricultural Campus in Truro/Bible Hill, Dalhousie is a truly national and international university, with more than half of our almost 21,000 students coming from outside of the province. Our 6,000 faculty and staff foster a vibrant, purpose-driven community, that celebrated 200 years of academic excellence in 2018. Job Summary Under the leadership of the Office of the Vice-President Research and Innovation, the Office of the University Veterinarian supports a vibrant research enterprise across multiple campuses and facilities, including animal-based research programs involving biomedical, agricultural, aquatic, and wildlife species. The Office of the University Veterinarian provides oversight and support for Dalhousie’s Animal Care and Use Program. This includes veterinary care, animal welfare oversight, regulatory compliance, post-approval monitoring, animal user training, support for Animal Care and Use Committees, and liaison with the Canadian Council on Animal Care ( CCAC ). Reporting to the University Veterinarian, the Associate University Veterinarian promotes and safeguards the ethical care and use of animals in research, teaching, and testing at Dalhousie University. The position provides veterinary expertise, animal welfare oversight, ethical review support, post-approval monitoring leadership, and clinical veterinary services in support of the University’s Animal Care and Use Program. Key Responsibilities Provide leadership for the University’s animal welfare and post-approval monitoring programs and promotes continuous improvement in the ethical care and use of animals. Provide veterinary consultation, clinical services, and welfare support for designated animal care and research programs. Support institutional ethical review and oversight processes and contributes to the strategic development of the Animal Care and Use Program. Contribute to the growth and development of animal care, animal welfare, and animal-based research and educational initiatives across Dalhousie University. Lead and coordinate the University’s Post-Approval Monitoring ( PAM ) Program, conducting risk-based audits and assessments of animal use activities and facilities. Note The successful applicant will be eligible for hybrid work (combination of in-person work on campus and remote work from within Nova Scotia) as agreed by all parties based on operational requirements and university guidelines. Required Qualifications Doctor of Veterinary Medicine ( DVM ) degree and eligible for licensure or currently licensed to practice veterinary medicine in Nova Scotia with a minimum five years of veterinary experience. Demonstrated experience applying veterinary knowledge and professional judgment to animal health, animal welfare, anesthesia, analgesia, surgery, clinical case management, and complex decision-making. Demonstrated experience in laboratory animal medicine, animal research oversight, institutional animal care and use programs, or university-based animal care and use programs is highly desirable and will be prioritized during candidate evaluation. Demonstrated experience providing veterinary consultation, training, mentorship, oversight, or guidance to animal users, students, technical staff, clients, researchers, or other stakeholders. Demonstrated experience working with laboratory animal species and research animal care and use programs is preferred. Experience with companion animal, agricultural, aquatic, wildlife, zoological, or mixed-animal practice may be considered relevant depending on the nature and extent of the experience. Demonstrated commitment to animal welfare, ethical animal use, evidence-based veterinary practice, and collaborative problem-solving is essential. Assets Experience with Canadian Council on Animal Care ( CCAC ) standards, animal ethics review, protocol review, post-approval monitoring, compliance activities, or institutional animal care and use programs is considered a strong asset. Job Competencies The successful candidate is expected to demonstrate proficiency in Dalhousie’s core and leadership competencies, in particular: - Respect & Inclusion - Adaptability - Knowledge & Thinking Skills - Communication - Service Additional Information Dalhousie University supports a healthy and balanced lifestyle. Our total compensation package includes a defined benefit pension plan, health and dental plans, a health spending account, an employee and family assistance program and a tuition assistance program. Application Consideration Applications from current university employees and external candidates are assessed concurrently. Current university employees will be given special consideration. We sincerely appreciate all applications and note that only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted directly by the hiring department or People and Culture. Diversity Statement Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Open Date 09/10/2026 Close Date 10/01/2026 Open Until Filled No Special Instructions to Applicant Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22115 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Optional Documents Cover Letter Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
