    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22146": {
      "title": "German DFA Instructor",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | German DFA Instructor Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants German DFA Instructor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title German DFA Instructor Posting Number F811P Type of position Probationary/Continuing Department/Unit German Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada About the opportunity The Dalhousie University Department of Languages, Literatures and Cultures seeks applications for a full-time probationary Instructor in German language and German studies, effective July 1, 2027. The position is subject to budgetary approval. The Instructor will primarily teach German language courses at all levels, as well as courses in the German Studies program. These are courses typically taught in English and cross-listed to also serve other programs in the department or faculty, on topics such as Confronting Fascism, Monsters & Madness (German Expressionist Film), German Romanticism, Modern German Drama, or Modern German Philosophy. This appointment supports the Department of Languages, Literatures and Cultures move toward a more integrated model of language teaching and learning and offers an opportunity to contribute to shared curriculum practices and student-centered initiatives during a period of program renewal and growth. The workload is equivalent to eight 3-credit hour courses during the one-year period, which would occasionally include two 3-credit courses taught in the summer term. Qualifications for the Position: Applicants should hold a Ph.D. in German studies (which could include comparative linguistics/literature/cultural studies/philosophy or related interdisciplinary fields) and a strong record of teaching German language classes. The ideal candidate will be able to invigorate course offerings and attract students to the program, building bridges between our curriculum and other programs at Dalhousie/King’s. Native or native-equivalent command of German and English, as well as extensive expertise in teaching the German language at the undergraduate level, are required. The successful candidate will possess strong organizational and administrative skills, and a commitment to fostering student engagement. Experience teaching in North American or European institutions will be considered an asset, as well as expertise in teaching German online. Applications must include the following: A letter of application addressed to the Search Committee; An up-to-date CV; A teaching dossier addressing teaching effectiveness (student evaluations, past syllabi etc.) Teaching statement/philosophy The Self-Identification Questionnaire (PeopleAdmin Portal) List of referees Please apply for this position directly via PeopleAdmin: https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22021 . For further information, please contact Lillian Sanderson, Administrative Coordinator, Department of Languages, Literatures, & Cultures, at L.Sanderson@dal.ca . This position is a part of the Dalhousie Faculty Association ( DFA ) Bargaining Unit. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Open Date 09/04/2026 Close Date 10/15/2026 Open Until Filled No Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22021 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover Letter Teaching Statement Teaching Dossier List of referees Optional Documents Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22148": {
      "title": "Dalhousie University",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Instructional Designer Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Instructional Designer Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Instructional Designer Research Project Department/Unit OLCD Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Posting Number S292-26 Employee Group DPMG Position Type Term Duration of Contract (if applicable) 2 years Employment Type Full Time Full-time Equivalency (FTE) 1.0 Salary $63,337 - $84,730 per annum, 35 hours per week Classification ADM-05 Provisional Statement This is a provisional classification. Formal regular review classification procedures may be initiated at any time by the incumbent or supervisor after the incumbent has worked a minimum of six months in the role. About Dalhousie University Dalhousie University is Atlantic Canada’s leading research-intensive university and a driver of the region’s intellectual, social and economic development. Located in the heart of Halifax, Nova Scotia, with an Agricultural Campus in Truro/Bible Hill, Dalhousie is a truly national and international university, with more than half of our almost 21,000 students coming from outside of the province. Our 6,000 faculty and staff foster a vibrant, purpose-driven community, that celebrated 200 years of academic excellence in 2018. Job Summary The Faculty of Open Learning and Career Development ( OLCD ) develops and delivers innovative and diverse lifelong learning experiences for more than 5000 students annually to help them navigate the changing world of learning and work. Reporting to the Program Director, the Instructional Designer will develop, refresh and build new courses and certificates for all program areas and subjects within OLCD , particularly courses for an online or hyflex (Hybrid & Flexible) delivery modality. Key Responsibilities Review the needs analysis and situation analysis conducted prior to the development of a new course to ensure there is adequate information for quality course development. Complete ongoing refresh and maintenance of existing courses and certificates to ensure they remain current. Develop and follow a schedule and work plan for the development and build of each course. Work collaboratively with Instructors and Subject Matter Experts (SMEs) to prepare course blueprints, outlines, learning outcomes, and assessments. Advise SMEs on inclusive pedagogical practices for diverse learners (cultural, linguistic, neurodiverse, and professional backgrounds). Develop complex learning activities with multi-media applications. Experiment with new course builds and activity ideas in a course sandbox. Maintain awareness of best practices and emerging technologies, and their ethical, pedagogical, and practical implications for course design. Designs and develops complex, interactive learning activities using multimedia authoring tools, including Articulate Storyline, Rise 360, and the Adobe Creative Suite. Complete quality control of courses to ensure they meet OLCD standards and to identify best practices, providing feedback as required, while completing course development and building work efficiently and cost effectively. Note The successful applicant will be eligible for hybrid work (combination of in-person work on campus and remote work from within Nova Scotia) as agreed by all parties based on operational requirements and university guidelines. Required Qualifications Undergraduate degree in education, curriculum studies or related field with approximately three to five years related progressive experience (or equivalent combination of training and experience). Experience in instructional design, curriculum development, multimedia design and/or educational technology. Experience with educational technologies, including learning management systems, design software, video and audio software, and presentation software. Must have experience using Articulate 360. A strong understanding of the principles of adult education. Exceptional interpersonal skills and the ability to build and maintain strong working relationships with a wide range of individuals. Strong attention to detail, with the ability to prioritize competing and shifting demands and organize complex tasks effectively. Assets Post-graduate degree/diploma in Instructional Design. Job Competencies The successful candidate is expected to demonstrate proficiency in Dalhousie’s core and leadership competencies, in particular: - Respect & Inclusion - Adaptability - Knowledge & Thinking Skills - Communication - Service Additional Information Dalhousie University supports a healthy and balanced lifestyle. Our total compensation package includes a defined benefit pension plan, health and dental plans, a health spending account, an employee and family assistance program and a tuition assistance program. Application Consideration Applications from current university employees and external candidates are assessed concurrently. Current university employees will be given special consideration. We sincerely appreciate all applications and note that only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted directly by the hiring department or People and Culture. Diversity Statement Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Open Date 09/08/2026 Close Date 09/22/2026 Open Until Filled No Special Instructions to Applicant Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22114 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Optional Documents Cover Letter Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22152": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Research Assistant Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Research Assistant Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Research Assistant Research Project Synthesis and Evaluation of New Chemical Entities Involving Conjugated Chemical Frameworks Department/Unit Chemistry Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Posting Number S288-26 Employee Group Grant-Paid (PSAC Local 86001) Position Type Term Duration of Contract (if applicable) 1 year Employment Type Part Time Full-time Equivalency (FTE) 0.2 Salary $51,526 - $65,227 per annum, $10,305 - $13,045 based on 0.2 FTE. Classification Provisional Statement About Dalhousie University Dalhousie University is Atlantic Canada’s leading research-intensive university and a driver of the region’s intellectual, social and economic development. Located in the heart of Halifax, Nova Scotia, with an Agricultural Campus in Truro/Bible Hill, Dalhousie is a truly national and international university, with more than half of our almost 21,000 students coming from outside of the province. Our 6,000 faculty and staff foster a vibrant, purpose-driven community, that celebrated 200 years of academic excellence in 2018. Job Summary The Thompson synthetic chemistry research group at Dalhousie University develops and evaluates technologies involving pyrroles and other conjugated chemical species for their potential as photo- and chemo-active materials. The group’s research is supported by external granting agencies and industry partners, and relies on strong financial, administrative, and project coordination support to optimize funding success, and to meet funder requirements and institutional timelines. Reporting to the Principal Investigator, the Research Assistant will provide dedicated research administration and coordination support to Dr. Thompson and the Thompson group, with a focus on financial management, budgeting, grant and funding administration, coordination of communications with internal units and external partners, and identifying and supporting applications for funding. Key Responsibilities Manage research and operating budgets, including financial tracking, reconciliations, invoicing, and reporting in compliance with granting agency and university requirements. Coordinate and contribute to grant administration activities, including funding proposals, progress reporting, milestone tracking, and deadline management. Provide comprehensive research administration support to facilitate research activities, projects, and personnel within the research group. Liaise with university departments, funding agencies, and external partners to support financial, administrative, payroll, and research-related processes. Prepare correspondence, reports, records, and other documentation to support research operations, funding requirements, and knowledge dissemination activities. Contribute to team effectiveness through recruitment support, promotion of an inclusive research environment, and active participation in group activities and university policies. Note The successful applicant will be eligible for hybrid work (combination of in-person work on campus and remote work from within Nova Scotia) as agreed by all parties based on operational requirements and university guidelines. Required Qualifications Undergraduate degree with a minimum five years of experience, in a research administration or executive administrative role supporting research in the chemical sciences. Minimum of five years of experience, beyond academic training, preparing funding proposals, managing budgets and reporting for/to Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada ( NSERC ) and a variety of other granting agencies that fund research in the chemical sciences. Minimum of five years of experience, beyond academic training, working with software used to support research administration and materials acquisition in the chemical sciences, e.g., MS Office (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, Teams, including spreadsheet construction and management, and scheduling), Chrome River, DalBuy. Minimum of five years of experience, beyond academic training, supporting research administration, training and procurement activities in the chemical sciences, including making recommendations on budget allocations, personnel-related matters and funding-related decision-making for consideration by the Principal Investigator. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively within multidisciplinary research environments and with researchers at various career stages in the chemical sciences, while effectively managing competing priorities and multiple projects. Strong attention to detail and commitment to accuracy in financial and confidential record-keeping, supported by excellent written and verbal communication skills in English. Assets Master’s degree in a science discipline Job Competencies The successful candidate is expected to demonstrate proficiency in Dalhousie’s core and leadership competencies, in particular: - Respect & Inclusion - Adaptability - Knowledge & Thinking Skills - Communication - Service Additional Information Application Consideration Non-probationary bargaining unit employees ( PSAC Local 86001) are given priority consideration. All other applications will be considered when there is no qualified candidate from the bargaining unit. Full-time students currently enrolled at an educational institution are not eligible for regular Grant-Paid positions. We sincerely appreciate all applications and note that only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted directly by the hiring department or People and Culture. Diversity Statement Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Open Date 09/03/2026 Close Date 09/14/2026 Open Until Filled No Special Instructions to Applicant Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22085 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Optional Documents Cover Letter Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22160": {
      "title": "Medical Device Reprocessing Technician",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Medical Device Reprocessing Technician Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Medical Device Reprocessing Technician Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Medical Device Reprocessing Technician Research Project Department/Unit Dental Clinic Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Posting Number S298-26 Employee Group None Position Type Temporary Duration of Contract (if applicable) 6 months Employment Type Full Time Full-time Equivalency (FTE) 1.0 Salary This is a temporary position for 6 months. Salary will commensurate with qualifications and experience ($26.14 - $33.09 per hour, 35 hours per week). Classification Provisional Statement About Dalhousie University Dalhousie University is Atlantic Canada’s leading research-intensive university and a driver of the region’s intellectual, social and economic development. Located in the heart of Halifax, Nova Scotia, with an Agricultural Campus in Truro/Bible Hill, Dalhousie is a truly national and international university, with more than half of our almost 21,000 students coming from outside of the province. Our 6,000 faculty and staff foster a vibrant, purpose-driven community, that celebrated 200 years of academic excellence in 2018. Job Summary The Faculty of Dentistry promotes and provides oral health care as an integral component of overall health for regional, national and international communities through quality education, research and service. Reporting to the Manager of Medical Device Reprocessing ( MDR ), the MDR Technician performs all duties associated with the collection and distribution of patient care items and the decontamination, inspection, assembly, packaging, sterilization handling, storage, picking and dispensing of patient care medical devices and supplies. Key Responsibilities Under minimal supervision, complete all phases of Dental Clinic sterilization process including picking of supplies for all dental treatments. Prioritize workload to meet clinical requirements of all Dental Clinic units for approximately 400 appointments daily. Check approximately 40 different tray setups, kits and packaged items. Remove excess consumable debris from tray set ups as required. Ensure all items are correctly assembled, packaged, sealed, identified and sorted for appropriate sterilization method. Complete testing procedures to ensure proper operation of sterilization equipment. Participate in monitoring stock of regular and consumable supplies, and partake in the annual inventory. Note Due to operational requirements, the successful applicant is required to work in-person on campus. Required Qualifications Registered Central Service Technician (Purdue University Correspondence Course, or equivalent), plus approximately one to three years’ of experience working in a health care or hospital environment. Ability to work both independently and as a member of a team. Organizational skills as well as the ability to multi-task and set priorities. The successful candidate must provide proof of up-to-date immunization for the following: Tetanus, Diphtheria, Polio, Measles, Mumps, Rubella, Chicken Pox and Hepatitis B. A copy of the lab report for Hepatitis B surface antibodies must be provided along with evidence of the 2 Step Mantoux (Tuberculin) test. Annual Influenza inoculation is recommended. The successful candidate must keep their immunization status current during their employment. Assets Job Competencies The successful candidate is expected to demonstrate proficiency in Dalhousie’s core competencies (https://www.dal.ca/dept/leaders.html), in particular: - Respect & Inclusion - Adaptability - Knowledge & Thinking Skills - Communication - Service Additional Information Application Consideration Applications from university employees and external candidates are given concurrent consideration. Among short listed candidates, university employees will be given special consideration. We thank all applicants for their interest, however, only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. Diversity Statement Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies One Open Date 09/09/2026 Close Date 09/23/2026 Open Until Filled No Special Instructions to Applicant Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22153 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Optional Documents Cover Letter Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22169": {
      "title": "Glider Technician I",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Glider Technician I Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Glider Technician I Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Glider Technician I Research Project The Ocean Tracking Network Department/Unit Biology Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Posting Number S290-26 Employee Group Grant-Paid (PSAC Local 86001) Position Type Term Duration of Contract (if applicable) 1 year with possibility of extension Employment Type Full Time Full-time Equivalency (FTE) 1.0 Salary $53,470 - $67,685 per annum, 37.5 hours per week Classification Provisional Statement About Dalhousie University Dalhousie University is Atlantic Canada’s leading research-intensive university and a driver of the region’s intellectual, social and economic development. Located in the heart of Halifax, Nova Scotia, with an Agricultural Campus in Truro/Bible Hill, Dalhousie is a truly national and international university, with more than half of our almost 21,000 students coming from outside of the province. Our 6,000 faculty and staff foster a vibrant, purpose-driven community, that celebrated 200 years of academic excellence in 2018. Job Summary The Ocean Tracking Network ( OTN , http://oceantrackingnetwork.org/) is a international marine and freshwater monitoring platform that focuses on tracking the movements of aquatic animals in the context of ocean conditions and climate change. Established in 2008, OTN is one of six Major Research Facilities supported by the Canada Foundation for Innovation ( CFI ). The OTN operates the largest academic fleet of uncrewed ocean-going vessels in Atlantic Canada. These vessels are used to observe, understand and predict physical, biogeochemical and ecological change in the ocean. Reporting to the Manager, Glider Operations, the Glider Technician I is a key part of the OTN glider team and helps ensure the smooth operation of glider activities. Key Responsibilities Assist with the planning, preparation, deployment, operation, and piloting of autonomous gliders for ocean-based research missions. Support at-sea deployment and recovery activities for gliders, small vessels, and associated scientific equipment. Perform maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of gliders and related field and laboratory equipment. Contribute to the testing, integration, and calibration of scientific sensors and instrumentation. Monitor and analyse glider performance and sensor data to optimize system functionality and data quality. Collect, manage, and document research data and project metadata, while coordinating with data managers and overseeing part-time glider pilots as required. Note Due to operational requirements, the successful applicant is required to work in-person from the COVE facility in Dartmouth. The position requires extended periods of travel (local, national, and international), and long days spent in the field and at sea. Candidates should be prepared to be at sea for multiple days at a time. Some evening, weekend, and holiday work will be required as well as routine glider pilot standby shifts. Required Qualifications Undergraduate degree in marine biology, oceanography, engineering or related field, with two years’ experience with ocean technology, acoustic telemetry, tagging, oceanographic data collection, or equivalent combination of training and experience (or an equivalent combination of training and experience). Demonstrated experience operating, maintaining, troubleshooting, and piloting autonomous underwater vehicles. Experience working in marine environments, including operating or supporting small vessel activities in a variety of sea conditions. Strong organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities, follow established protocols, maintain accurate documentation, and adhere to operational procedures. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively with diverse stakeholders including industry partners, vessel operators, researchers, and community members. Proven problem-solving abilities and adaptability in dynamic field environments, with the ability to travel internationally, hold a valid driver’s licence, meet marine medical requirements, and safely lift equipment weighing over 20 kg. Assets Experience overseeing glider missions from start to finish Understanding of acoustic technology and research, including study design, mission planning, and field operations. General knowledge of oceanographic sensors, such as Conductivity, Temperature, and Depth (CTDs), oxygen sensors, hydrophones, echosounders and data loggers. Experience with spatial mapping programs, GPS technology, plotting coordinates, and navigation. Conversational French language skills Experience towing a trailer. Hold Transport Canada certificates of completion for Small Vessel Operator Proficiency ( SVOP ) and Small Non-pleasure Domestic Vessel Basic Safety ( SDV -BS) or MED -A3. Job Competencies The successful candidate is expected to demonstrate proficiency in Dalhousie’s core and leadership competencies, in particular: - Respect & Inclusion - Adaptability - Knowledge & Thinking Skills - Communication - Service Additional Information Dalhousie University supports a healthy and balanced lifestyle. Our total compensation package includes a voluntary RRSP , health and dental plans, a wellness allowance, an employee and family assistance program and a tuition assistance program. Application Consideration Non-probationary bargaining unit employees ( PSAC Local 86001) are given priority consideration. All other applications will be considered when there is no qualified candidate from the bargaining unit. Full-time students currently enrolled at an educational institution are not eligible for regular Grant-Paid positions. We sincerely appreciate all applications and note that only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted directly by the hiring department or People and Culture. Diversity Statement Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Open Date 09/10/2026 Close Date 09/17/2026 Open Until Filled No Special Instructions to Applicant Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22108 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Optional Documents Cover Letter Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
